<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation-laptop.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/learning-center/">Learning center</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Attorney: Who Should You Call?</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Guide · 6 min read</p><h1>Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Attorney: Who Should You Call?</h1><p class="lede">Three professionals help policyholders with property claims, and their roles overlap less than you might think. Here’s how to pick the right one.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Minnesota Insurance Appraiser team · Published <time datetime="2026-10-27">October 27, 2026</time> · 6 min read</p>
<p>When a property claim goes sideways, homeowners often hear three suggestions at once: hire a public adjuster, invoke appraisal, or call a lawyer. Each professional plays a different role, is paid differently and fits a different stage of a dispute. Choosing the wrong one can cost time and money. This article lays out the differences as they generally apply in Minnesota, so you can decide which conversation to have first.</p>

<h2>The insurance appraiser</h2>
<p>An appraiser is selected under the appraisal provision of the policy, which in Minnesota follows the structure in the standard fire policy statute, Minn. Stat. § 65A.01. The appraiser’s job is narrow: determine the amount of a covered loss and negotiate that amount with the insurer’s appraiser, with an umpire breaking any deadlock.</p>
<h3>Key traits</h3>
<ul>
<li>Focuses on valuation: measurements, scope and pricing</li>
<li>Must be competent and disinterested, so fees are usually hourly or flat rather than a share of the award</li>
<li>Does not decide coverage questions or give legal advice</li>
<li>Works within a defined process that ends with a signed award</li>
</ul>
<p>Appraisal fits best when the insurer has accepted the claim but the dollar amount is in dispute.</p>

<h2>The public adjuster</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder in preparing, presenting and negotiating the claim with the insurer. In Minnesota, public adjusters are licensed by the Department of Commerce, and their contracts and fees are subject to state rules.</p>
<h3>Key traits</h3>
<ul>
<li>Handles the claim from early stages: documentation, inventory and negotiation</li>
<li>Often paid a percentage of the claim payment, subject to state requirements</li>
<li>Can be helpful on large or complex losses where the policyholder lacks time or expertise</li>
<li>Does not give legal advice and cannot represent you in court</li>
</ul>
<p>Because public adjusters often work on a percentage, some policies and courts question whether they can also serve as the “disinterested” appraiser in the same claim. Ask about that directly if you are considering one.</p>

<h2>The attorney</h2>
<p>An attorney is the right call when the dispute is about legal rights rather than numbers. That includes outright denials, coverage interpretation, missed or approaching deadlines and claims that an insurer acted unreasonably.</p>
<h3>Key traits</h3>
<ul>
<li>Advises on coverage, policy interpretation and deadlines</li>
<li>Can file suit and represent you in court</li>
<li>Can evaluate remedies such as those under Minn. Stat. § 604.18, which allows a court to award certain amounts when an insurer denies benefits without a reasonable basis, subject to procedural requirements</li>
<li>Fee arrangements vary, including hourly and contingency</li>
</ul>

<h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Appraiser</th><th>Public adjuster</th><th>Attorney</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Main focus</td><td>Amount of loss</td><td>Preparing and negotiating the claim</td><td>Legal rights and coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensed by Minnesota Department of Commerce</td><td>Not as a public adjuster</td><td>Yes</td><td>No; licensed by the courts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can give legal advice</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical stage</td><td>After a valuation dispute forms</td><td>Early, or throughout the claim</td><td>Denial, coverage issues, deadlines</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>How to decide</h2>
<p>Ask yourself a few questions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Has the insurer accepted the claim?</strong> If yes and you disagree only on the amount, appraisal is worth considering.</li>
<li><strong>Has the insurer denied coverage?</strong> If yes, talk to an attorney first.</li>
<li><strong>Are you overwhelmed by the claim itself?</strong> If the loss is large and you need help documenting and presenting it, a public adjuster may fit.</li>
<li><strong>Is a deadline approaching?</strong> Standard policy language commonly limits suits to two years after the loss. Confirm in your policy and with an attorney.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Questions to ask before you hire anyone</h2>
<ul>
<li>What exactly will you do on my claim, and what will you not do?</li>
<li>How are you paid, and can I see the fee agreement in writing before I sign?</li>
<li>Do you have any relationship with my contractor or my insurer?</li>
<li>Have you handled this type of loss, such as hail on steel siding or an ice dam interior claim?</li>
<li>Who else might I need, and when would you tell me so?</li>
</ul>
<p>A professional who answers these plainly is usually a good sign. Be cautious with anyone who promises a specific result or pressures you to sign at the door after a storm. The Minnesota Department of Commerce publishes license lookups for public adjusters, and the Minnesota State Bar Association can help you find an attorney.</p>
<p>These roles can work together. An attorney may advise on coverage while an appraiser handles valuation. A policyholder who used a public adjuster early may later go to appraisal with a separate, disinterested appraiser.</p>

<h2>Where we fit</h2>
<p>Minnesota Insurance Appraiser is an independent appraisal firm. We are not lawyers and not public adjusters. We review estimates, serve as the policyholder’s appraiser under the appraisal clause and act as umpire when selected. If your claim needs a lawyer or a public adjuster instead, we will say so. Start with our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/appraisal-clause-help/">appraisal clause help</a> page, read about <a href="/appraisal-solutions/appraisal-umpire/">umpire services</a>, or <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a> for a first look.</p>
